description:
Write the two pieces between lesson 6.03's worker thread and its window: a throttle that decides when to post, and a batch that collects results in between. Time is given in milliseconds by the caller so the logic can be tested:

```cpp
class Throttle
{
public:
    explicit Throttle(int intervalMs);
    bool ready(long long nowMs);       // true at most once per interval; the first call is always ready
    bool finish();                     // true if anything was held back since the last time ready() was true
    void held();                       // something happened that was not reported yet
};

template <typename T>
class Batch
{
public:
    bool add(T item);                  // true if the window must be told (no message is pending)
    std::vector<T> take();             // the UI thread takes everything; the next add() will ask again
    std::size_t pending() const;
};
```

**Throttle**: `ready(now)` returns true if this is the first call, or at least `intervalMs` have passed since the last time it returned true; then it also forgets anything held. `held()` marks that something was not reported; `finish()` returns whether such a thing is still unreported (and clears it), so the final state is always shown when the search ends.

**Batch** (used from two threads, so protect it with a mutex): `add` appends the item and returns true only if no "new results" message is pending yet, and then marks one as pending; `take` returns all items, clears the batch and the pending mark. So however many items arrive, at most one message is in the queue.

